.TH WM-OSK 1
.SH NAME
wm/osk \- a keyboard on the screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/osk
[
.B -sm
] [
.B -M
.BR text | plain | secret
] [
.B -l
.I layout
] [
.B -F
.I fd
] [
.B -r
.I tracefile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Osk
draws a keyboard along the bottom of the screen, to be typed on with
fingers.  Its keys type into whatever has the keyboard, as a keyboard's
do (through
.BR /dev/kbdin ,
.IR cons (3)),
and it never takes the keyboard itself: it asks the window manager for
the pointer only and to be docked, and
.IR wm (1)
and Lucifer give it a strip of its own, the windows (or Lucifer's
zones) above it.
.PP
A key types when the finger lifts: the key under it then.  A finger
that lands on the wrong key slides to the right one; the key under a
finger is shown large above it.  Two fingers may be down at once (two
thumbs, one key still held as the next is touched): each is its own
touch
.RB ( /dev/fingers ,
.IR pointer (3)).
A mouse's clicks type too.
.PP
As a word is typed, the likeliest words for its touches are shown above
the keys
.RI ( kbdecode (2)):
the touches against the keys, where a finger lands and how it slips,
and a word list
.RB ( /lib/osk/en.words ,
and the words learned).  When the word ends (a space, punctuation, a
new line) a slip onto the next key, a letter missed or doubled, two
swapped, or an apostrophe left out
.RB ( dont )
is put right, if the word meant is clearly likelier than what was
typed; a word in the list, a single letter, one with a digit is left.
A delete straight after takes the correction back, and the word is not
put right again.  The first of the words shown is what was typed, in
quotes; the one a space would put in is in the accent colour; a touch on
one puts it in.  Two spaces after a word end a sentence (a full stop),
and a sentence's first letter is a capital.
.PP
A stroke through a word's letters, without lifting, types the word
(a swipe), with a space before it after another word; the next likeliest
are shown, and a touch on one puts it in its place.  A delete straight
after a swiped word deletes it whole.
.PP
The keys of its own:
.TF shift
.TP
.B shift
the next letter a capital; twice quickly, capitals until shift again
.TP
.B del
delete; held, it repeats, and after a while deletes words.  A stroke
left from it deletes a word for every three keys' width.
.TP
.B ?123
numbers and symbols, and from them
.B =\e<
more: Esc, Tab, Ctrl (the next key a control character), the arrows,
Home and End;
.B abc
the letters again
.TP
.I globe
the next letters' layout
.TP
.I space
shows the layout's name
.PD
.PP
A long press on a key offers its accents and alternates
.RB ( /lib/osk/alternates );
the finger slides to one and lifts.  A swipe down from above the keys,
or a touch on the keyboard glyph at the right of the words, hides the
keyboard to a handle along the bottom; a touch on the handle shows it.
.PP
The layouts are QWERTY, Dvorak and Colemak, the letters, and the
symbols, each a text file in
.B /lib/osk/layouts
(the format is at the head of
.BR /lib/osk/layouts/dvorak );
.B /lib/osk/order
lists the letters' layouts in the order the globe key goes through them.
The layout last used is kept in
.BR $home/lib/osk/layout ,
the words learned in
.BR $home/lib/osk/words ,
and where the typist's touches land on each key, learned as words are
typed and not taken back, in
.BR $home/lib/osk/offsets. \fIlayout\fR.
A word not in the list that is typed and kept twice is learned, as is a
word whose correction was taken back and that is then kept.
.PP
Without
.BR -s ,
.I osk
is shown from the start, whatever else is attached: it was asked for.
With
.B -s
it is the system's keyboard, as Lucifer starts it: hidden while a
keyboard of the machine's is attached
.RB ( /dev/keyboards ,
less the devices
.B /lib/osk/ignore
names that only call themselves keyboards) or has just been typed on,
and otherwise shown once the screen is touched.  With
.B -m
as well (Lucifer on a phone or tablet), it is shown only when asked
(a text field taking the keyboard asks, through
.IR softkbd (2)).
Lucifer starts it unless the environment variable
.B osk
is
.BR off ;
on iOS and Android the system's keyboard is never shown, whether this
runs or not, and a keyboard attached to the device types as it always
did.
.PP
.B -M
sets the mode
.IR osk (4)'s
.B mode
request sets:
.B text
(prose: words put right, sentences begun with a capital),
.B plain
(a shell's or code's: words offered, never put right) or
.B secret
(a password's: nothing put right, nothing shown above the keys or
enlarged under the finger, nothing learned, and a trace says only that
a key was typed).  The boot starts
.B wm/osk -s -M secret
for the login screen, with no window manager: it draws along the
bottom of the screen and takes only the touches there, and is killed
when the login is done, Lucifer starting its own.
.PP
.B -l
.I layout
starts with that letters' layout.
.B -F
.I fd
serves
.IR osk (4)'s
files on file descriptor
.I fd
(for the program that started it to mount) instead of mounting them at
.BR /mnt/osk .
.B -r
.I tracefile
records every touch, what was typed for it and every word put right or
swiped, a line each, from the start (as
.B record
on its ctl file does): the traces a typist's own typing makes, to test
and tune it by.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/osk/layouts/*
.TP
.B /lib/osk/layouts/*
the layouts
.TP
.B /lib/osk/en.words
the word list: SCOWL's words, counted in Google Books Ngram
.RB ( /lib/osk/NOTICE ;
.B tools/osk/mkwords.sh
makes it)
.TP
.B /lib/lucifer/theme/*
its colours: a theme's roles (header, input, border, accent, text),
unless the theme sets the keyboard's own,
.BR osk-board ,
.BR osk-key ,
.B osk-meta
(shift, delete, the layers),
.BR osk-text ,
.BR osk-metatext ,
.B osk-press
(a key held down),
.BR osk-presstext ,
.B osk-word
(the words shown),
.B osk-auto
and
.B osk-autobg
(the word a space would put in, and behind it), as Glenda does
.TP
.B $home/lib/osk/*
what it has learned
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/osk.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/kbdecode.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/osklayout.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wm (1),
.IR kbdecode (2),
.IR softkbd (2),
.IR cons (3),
.IR pointer (3),
.IR osk (4)
.SH BUGS
It knows only what it typed: where the caret is, and what is in front of
it, are the application's.  A touch elsewhere, a key of the machine's,
an arrow key, and the word being typed is forgotten, and the next delete
is a plain one.
.br
English only.
